 @antiwokehuman   Otherwise good but  the core size difference is mostly explained by the manufacturing technology. The transistor count is almost the same (26,8 vs 28,3 billion transistors).

It's a problem with architecture if they're forced to run super high clockspeeds to match a similar transistor count Ampere card in performance. Not to mention the Ampere cards have more Tensor & RT cores which take up more space and transistors than on the Radeons.

RDNA2 isn't bad, it's a huge step from RDNA1 but still a bit lacking in efficiency. Considering their path I give them a good chance to catch up with NVIDIA to a point where there's next to no difference, but currently they're only competing thanks to having a superior node.

 @setzer-j6s  According to the Hardware unboxed review of the 6800M, it matches the 3080 mobile in performance. I didn't catch any power charts, but the 6800M has a wider power spectrum 110-160W vs the 130-150W for the 3080 laptop. It's not as much about "different ways to measure" but as allowing adjustment to fit within the laptop's cooling budget.

A wide power spectrum is an ass on itself though as the wider it gets the more your average consumer might get screwed by buying an expensive GPU that will be outperformed or matched by a cheaper one with better cooling.

Edit: LTT ran a 3080 mobile (130 watt) vs 6800M (115watt) and the results were heavily skewed towards the 3080 mobile. More than the power difference would explain. This might be selection bias, but it shows that it's definitely not clearly ahead of the 3080 mobile despite having a superior fabrication node.

